    {
      "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20y",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "20Y Destruction of credit reporting information in cases of fraud",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012#sch-2-sec-20y"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf, p.103",
      "values:originalText": "(1) This section applies if:\n\n(a) a credit reporting body holds credit reporting information\nabout an individual; and\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 2  Credit reporting\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\n(b) the information relates to consumer credit that has been\nprovided by a credit provider to the individual, or a person\npurporting to be the individual; and\n\n(c) the body is satisfied that:\n\n(i) the individual has been a victim of fraud (including\nidentity fraud); and\n\n(ii) the consumer credit was provided as a result of that\nfraud.\nDestruction of credit reporting information\n\n(2) The credit reporting body must:\n\n(a) destroy the credit reporting information; and\n\n(b) within a reasonable period after the information is destroyed:\n\n(i) give the individual a written notice that states that the\ninformation has been destroyed and sets out the effect of\nsubsection (4); and\n\n(ii) give the credit provider a written notice that states that\nthe information has been destroyed.\nCivil penalty:\n1,000 penalty units.\n\n(3) Subsection (2) does not apply if the credit reporting body is\nrequired by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order, to\nretain the credit reporting information.\nNotification of destruction to third parties\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) a credit reporting body destroys credit reporting information\nabout an individual under subsection (2); and\n\n(b) the body has previously disclosed the information to one or\nmore recipients under Subdivision D of this Division;\nthe body must, within a reasonable period after the destruction,\nnotify those recipients of the destruction and the matters referred to\nin paragraph (1)(c).\nCivil penalty:\n500 penalty units.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nCredit reporting  Schedule 2\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\n(5) Subsection (4) does not apply if the credit reporting body is\nrequired by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order,\nnot to give the notification.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) This section applies if:\n\n(a) a credit reporting body holds credit reporting information\nabout an individual; and\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 2  Credit reporting\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\n(b) the information relates to consumer credit that has been\nprovided by a credit provider to the individual, or a person\npurporting to be the individual; and\n\n(c) the body is satisfied that:\n\n(i) the individual has been a victim of fraud (including\nidentity fraud); and\n\n(ii) the consumer credit was provided as a result of that\nfraud.\nDestruction of credit reporting information\n\n(2) The credit reporting body must:\n\n(a) destroy the credit reporting information; and\n\n(b) within a reasonable period after the information is destroyed:\n\n(i) give the individual a written notice that states that the\ninformation has been destroyed and sets out the effect of\nsubsection (4); and\n\n(ii) give the credit provider a written notice that states that\nthe information has been destroyed.\nCivil penalty:\n1,000 penalty units.\n\n(3) Subsection (2) does not apply if the credit reporting body is\nrequired by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order, to\nretain the credit reporting information.\nNotification of destruction to third parties\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) a credit reporting body destroys credit reporting information\nabout an individual under subsection (2); and\n\n(b) the body has previously disclosed the information to one or\nmore recipients under Subdivision D of this Division;\nthe body must, within a reasonable period after the destruction,\nnotify those recipients of the destruction and the matters referred to\nin paragraph (1)(c).\nCivil penalty:\n500 penalty units.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nCredit reporting  Schedule 2\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\n(5) Subsection (4) does not apply if the credit reporting body is\nr",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20y-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20y-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20y-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20y-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20y-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8bbdc0db1469e8cfe0b64aaa3604301ac2ab9ce9da0acd64c926268d0606398a"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012#sch-2-sec-20y",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "20Y Destruction of credit reporting information in cases of fraud",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 103,
      "values:originalText": "(1) This section applies if:\n\n(a) a credit reporting body holds credit reporting information\nabout an individual; and\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 2  Credit reporting\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\n(b) the information relates to consumer credit that has been\nprovided by a credit provider to the individual, or a person\npurporting to be the individual; and\n\n(c) the body is satisfied that:\n\n(i) the individual has been a victim of fraud (including\nidentity fraud); and\n\n(ii) the consumer credit was provided as a result of that\nfraud.\nDestruction of credit reporting information\n\n(2) The credit reporting body must:\n\n(a) destroy the credit reporting information; and\n\n(b) within a reasonable period after the information is destroyed:\n\n(i) give the individual a written notice that states that the\ninformation has been destroyed and sets out the effect of\nsubsection (4); and\n\n(ii) give the credit provider a written notice that states that\nthe information has been destroyed.\nCivil penalty:\n1,000 penalty units.\n\n(3) Subsection (2) does not apply if the credit reporting body is\nrequired by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order, to\nretain the credit reporting information.\nNotification of destruction to third parties\n\n(4) If:\n\n(a) a credit reporting body destroys credit reporting information\nabout an individual under subsection (2); and\n\n(b) the body has previously disclosed the information to one or\nmore recipients under Subdivision D of this Division;\nthe body must, within a reasonable period after the destruction,\nnotify those recipients of the destruction and the matters referred to\nin paragraph (1)(c).\nCivil penalty:\n500 penalty units.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nCredit reporting  Schedule 2\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\n(5) Subsection (4) does not apply if the credit reporting body is\nrequired by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order,\nnot to give the notification."
    },
    {
