    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-21s",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "21S Security of credit eligibility information",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012#sch-2-sec-21s"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf, p.127",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If a credit provider holds credit eligibility information, the provider\nmust take such steps as are reasonable in the circumstances to\nprotect the information:\n\n(a) from misuse, interference and loss; and\n\n(b) from unauthorised access, modification or disclosure.\n\n(2) If:\n\n(a) a credit provider holds credit eligibility information about an\nindividual; and\n\n(b) the provider no longer needs the information for any purpose\nfor which the information may be used or disclosed by the\nprovider under this Division; and\n\n(c) the provider is not required by or under an Australian law, or\na court/tribunal order, to retain the information;\nthe provider must take such steps as are reasonable in the\ncircumstances to destroy the information or to ensure that the\ninformation is de-identified.\nCivil penalty:\n1,000 penalty units.\n\n(3) If a credit provider is an APP entity, Australian Privacy Principle\n11 does not apply to the provider in relation to credit eligibility\ninformation.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 2  Credit reporting\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\nSubdivision F—Access to, and correction of, information",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If a credit provider holds credit eligibility information, the provider\nmust take such steps as are reasonable in the circumstances to\nprotect the information:\n\n(a) from misuse, interference and loss; and\n\n(b) from unauthorised access, modification or disclosure.\n\n(2) If:\n\n(a) a credit provider holds credit eligibility information about an\nindividual; and\n\n(b) the provider no longer needs the information for any purpose\nfor which the information may be used or disclosed by the\nprovider under this Division; and\n\n(c) the provider is not required by or under an Australian law, or\na court/tribunal order, to retain the information;\nthe provider must take such steps as are reasonable in the\ncircumstances to destroy the information or to ensure that the\ninformation is de-identified.\nCivil penalty:\n1,000 penalty units.\n\n(3) If a credit provider is an APP entity, Australian Privacy Principle\n11 does not apply to the provider in relation to credit eligibility\ninformation.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 2  Credit reporting\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\nSubdivision F—Access to, and correction of, information",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-21s-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-21s-ss-2"
        },
