    {
      "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-4-sec-98a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "98A Treatment of partnerships",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012#sch-4-sec-98a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf, p.213",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If, apart from this subsection, this Act would impose an obligation\non a partnership, the obligation is imposed instead on each partner\nbut may be discharged by any of the partners.\n\n(2) If, apart from this subsection, an offence against this Act would be\ncommitted by a partnership, the offence is taken to have been\ncommitted by each partner.\n\n(3) If, apart from this subsection, a partnership would contravene a\ncivil penalty provision, the contravention is taken to have been\ncommitted by each partner.\n\n(4) A partner does not commit an offence against this Act because of\nsubsection (2), or contravene a civil penalty provision because of\nsubsection (3), if the partner:\n\n(a) does not know of the circumstances that constitute the\ncontravention of the provision concerned; or\n\n(b) knows of those circumstances but takes all reasonable steps\nto correct the contravention as soon as possible after the\npartner becomes aware of those circumstances.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 4  Other amendments of the Privacy Act 1988\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\nNote:\nIn criminal proceedings, a defendant bears an evidential burden in\nrelation to the matters in subsection (4) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the\nCriminal Code).",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If, apart from this subsection, this Act would impose an obligation\non a partnership, the obligation is imposed instead on each partner\nbut may be discharged by any of the partners.\n\n(2) If, apart from this subsection, an offence against this Act would be\ncommitted by a partnership, the offence is taken to have been\ncommitted by each partner.\n\n(3) If, apart from this subsection, a partnership would contravene a\ncivil penalty provision, the contravention is taken to have been\ncommitted by each partner.\n\n(4) A partner does not commit an offence against this Act because of\nsubsection (2), or contravene a civil penalty provision because of\nsubsection (3), if the partner:\n\n(a) does not know of the circumstances that constitute the\ncontravention of the provision concerned; or\n\n(b) knows of those circumstances but takes all reasonable steps\nto correct the contravention as soon as possible after the\npartner becomes aware of those circumstances.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 4  Other amendments of the Privacy Act 1988\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\nNote:\nIn criminal proceedings, a defendant bears an evidential burden in\nrelation to the matters in subsection (4) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the\nCriminal Code).",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-4-sec-98a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-4-sec-98a-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-4-sec-98a-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-4-sec-98a-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f2361ef30a00e9000f3f64cdbfd92a7f6679617f3c4e1c9bb2cdca5f5a245dc0"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012#sch-4-sec-98a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "98A Treatment of partnerships",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 213,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If, apart from this subsection, this Act would impose an obligation\non a partnership, the obligation is imposed instead on each partner\nbut may be discharged by any of the partners.\n\n(2) If, apart from this subsection, an offence against this Act would be\ncommitted by a partnership, the offence is taken to have been\ncommitted by each partner.\n\n(3) If, apart from this subsection, a partnership would contravene a\ncivil penalty provision, the contravention is taken to have been\ncommitted by each partner.\n\n(4) A partner does not commit an offence against this Act because of\nsubsection (2), or contravene a civil penalty provision because of\nsubsection (3), if the partner:\n\n(a) does not know of the circumstances that constitute the\ncontravention of the provision concerned; or\n\n(b) knows of those circumstances but takes all reasonable steps\nto correct the contravention as soon as possible after the\npartner becomes aware of those circumstances.\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053\n\nSchedule 4  Other amendments of the Privacy Act 1988\n\nPrivacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012\n\nCompilation date: 12/3/14\nRegistered: 6/3/15\n\nNote:\nIn criminal proceedings, a defendant bears an evidential burden in\nrelation to the matters in subsection (4) (see subsection 13.3(3) of the\nCriminal Code)."
    },
    {
